Target: Medirozahospital.com
(Authorized target confirm scope before publishing)

Tools used: whois, theHarvester, subfinder, nslookup, whatweb and nmap.

Enviroment: Kali LinuxVM.

#Reconnaissance: whois
Command: whois medirozahospital.com

Findings
Registrar: NameCheap, Inc
Domain created: 14/08/2026
Domain expires: 14/08/2027 (recently registered)

Name servers:
DNS1.NAMECHEAPHOSTING.COM,
DNS2.NAMECHEAPHOSTING.COM

DNSSEC: unsigned

Status: clientTransferProhibited (transfer lock enabled)

Abuse contact: abuse@namecheap.com

Takeaway:
DNS and likely hosting are through NNamecheap, which tells me where to look next.
DNSSEC is unsigned, so DNS responses aren’t cryptographically validated (a minor hardening gap)
The domain is only weeks old, which is worth noting for.a hospital site.

#Subdomain and email Enumeration: theHarverster
Command: theHarvester -d medirozahospital.com -b crtsh

Source: crt.sh (certificate transparency logs, passive)
Results: No IPs, emails,people or hosts found.

Takeaway: 
No certificate transparency records exist for this domain via crt.sh, so no subdomains or email addresses could be enumerated this way. this consistent with the domain being newly registered (Aug 2026).

#Subdomain Enumaration: Subfinder
Command: subfinder -d medirozahospital.com

Results: Found 1 subdomain in 10.7 seconds.
www.medirozahospital.com

Takeaway:
Only standard www subdomain was discoverable through subfinders passive sources. No additional subdomains

#DNS Resolution: nslookup 
Command: nslookup medirozahospital.com 

Results: medirozahospital.com resolves to 199.188.201.16

Takeaway:
Confirms the live IP address hosting the site, used for further port/service scanning below.

#DNS Enumeration: dnsrecon
Command: dnsrecon -d medirozahospital.com

Results: 12 DNS records found 

Name servers: dns1/dns2.namecheaphosting.com
DNSSEC: No answer to DNSSEC query (unsigned, consistent with the whois finding)
Bind version: disclosed as "Nameserver" string on both nameservers
A record: medirozahospital.com - 199.188.201.16
MX records: mx1/mx2/mx3-hosting.jelly.systems (162.255.118.28/.297.13)
SPF(TXT): *v=spf1 +a +mx +ip4:199:188:201:16.7 +ip4:199.188.201.38 include:spf.web-hosting.com -all
DMARC(TXT): v=DMARC; p=none; policy set to "none" meaning spoofed mail isn’t rejected or quarantined
SRV records:CalDAV/CaedDav on medirozahospital.com (199.188.201.16), and autodiscover pointing to cPanel email discovery service (cpanelemaildiscovery.cpanel.net)across 6 IPs

Takeway: Mail infrastructure runs through ePanel based hosting (jellyfish.systems/web hosting.com), seperate from the main site. The DMARC policy of p=none is a real finding, it means the domain doesn’t instruct receiving mail servers to reject or quarantine spoofed emails claiming to be from medirozahospital.com, which is  a phishing risk against staff or patients.

Recommendation: Tighten DMARC policy to p=quarantine or p=reject once SPF/DKIM alignment is confirmed to reduce email spoofing risk.

#Web server and Technology Fingerprinting Curl
Command: curl -i -L medirozahospital.com

Results:
Sitebredirects HTTP HTTPS (301,then 200 ok)
Server: LifeSpeed
CMS identified via met tag: Mediroza CMS 1.4.2
Author meta tag reveals internal team name: "Mediroza IT department"
Two authentication endpoints discovered in page source:
                                                        /staff/login.php (staff login)
                                                       /patient/login.php (patient portal login)
Takeaway: The generator meta tag exposes the axact CMS and version in use (mediroza CMS 1.4.2), useful for an attcker to look up known vulnerabilities for that version.Response headres cornfirm LiteSpeed as the web server. Two login portals were identified as potential targets for further testing (brute force protection default credentials), subject to authorized scope.
  
  Recommendation : Remove or genericize the generator and author meta tags in production so the CMS name/version isn’t public disclosed.

#Technology Fingerprinting whatweb
Command: whatweb medirozahospital.com

Results:
http://medirozahospital.com 301 Moved Permanently redirects to https
https://medirozahospital.com 403 Forbidden
Server: LiteSpeed,HTTP/1.1
country: United States
Uncommon header:x turbo charged by

Takeaway: whatweb received a 403 Forbidden on the HTTPS site, while the earlier curl -i -L request returned a full 200 OK with page content. this suggest the server or a WAF is filtering requests based on User-Agent or request signature. whatweb default request was blocked where curl wasn’t. This is itself a finding basic bot/scanner filtering is in place though inconsistency applied since a slightly different request by passwd it

#Port Scanning and Service Detection Nmap
Command nmap -sV medirozahospital.com

Results:
Host: medirozahospital.com
DNS: server247-1.webhosting.com
998 ports filtered (no response), 2 open:
                                         - 80/tcp http OpenResty web app server 1.31.1.1
                                         - 443/tcp ssl/http OpenResty web app server 1.31.1.1

Takeaway: Nmap identifies the server as OpenResty which conflicts with the LiteSoeed server header see in thr curl and whatweb results. This suggests a reverse proxy or CDN layer (OpenResty) sits in front of the actual origin server. The reverse DNS server 274-1.webhosting.com confirms shared hosting on Namecheap/webhosting.com infrastructure. Only ports 80/443 are exposed, all other ports are filtered which is good security practice. No unnecessary services are reachable from the internet.

#Summary Of Key Findings


 | Finding | Severity |
|---------|----------|
| DNSSEC unsigned | Low |
| CMS version disclosed (Mediroza CMS 1.4.2) via meta tag | Low/Medium |
| DMARC policy set to p=none | Medium |
| Inconsistent WAF/bot filtering (whatweb blocked, curl allowed) | Informational |
| Reverse proxy (OpenResty) masks true origin server (LiteSpeed) | Informational |
| Only ports 80/443 exposed | Positive |


#Week 4 - Penetration Test Project

Target:medirozahospital.com (authorized training target — Network Walks internship, group B083D)

Tools used: Burp Suite, pdfcrack, curl
Environment: Kali Linux VM


#Authentication Bypass — SQL Injection (Burp Suite Intruder)

Target:/patient/login.php
Payload:admin'%20-- in the username field

Result: Returned a 302 redirect to portal.php with a response length of 335 bytes, distinct from all failed-login responses (~2770+ bytes).

Analysis: The '-- sequence comments out the rest of the SQL query, likely bypassing the password check entirely for the admin user.

Impact: Full authentication bypass on the patient portal.

Recommendation:** Use parameterized queries for all authentication logic, validate input server-side, implement login rate limiting and lockout, and log/alert on SQL metacharacters in auth fields.


#Post-Bypass Access — Exposed Patient Lab Reports

URL:/patient/portal.php (reached via the bypass in Finding 1)

Result: The portal listed 3 downloadable, password-protected lab reports.

Takeaway: A single SQL injection flaw led directly to unauthenticated access to what should be private patient health records.

Recommendation: Enforce authorization checks server-side on every report/file request, not just at login, so one auth bypass doesn't cascade into full data exposure.


#Directory Listing Enabled

URLs:/patient/ and /staff/

Result: Both directories have browsing enabled, exposing internal filenames download.php error_log login.php logout.php portal.php

Takeaway: Directory indexing reveals internal structure and filenames that help plan further attacks; an exposed error_log could also leak debug information.

Recommendation: Disable directory listing on the web server, and move files like error_log outside the web root.


#Weak PDF Encryption Password — pdfcrack

Command: pdfcrack -f patient_report_2.pdf -w rockyou.txt

Result: Password cracked almost instantly: "password"

Takeaway: The PDF's encryption may be standards-compliant, but it's meaningless with a trivially guessable password.

Recommendation: Enforce strong, randomly generated passwords for any password-protected documents; consider authenticated document delivery instead of password-protected PDFs.


#Robots.txt Disclosure

Command: curl -s https://medirozahospital.com/robots.txt

Result:
User-agent: 
Disallow: /patient/
Disallow: /staff/
Disallow: /old/
Sitemap: https://medirozahospital.com/sitemap.xml


Takeaway: robots.txt is meant to guide crawlers, not restrict access — but by listing /patient/, /staff/, and /old/, it effectively advertises sensitive paths to investigate, including a previously undiscovered /old/ path.

Recommendation: Don't rely on robots.txt for access control. Enforce authentication/authorization on these paths server-side, and review whether the /old/ directory is still needed.


#Legacy File Exposure (details withheld)

A legacy file was discovered at a path disclosed via robots.txt (Finding 5), publicly accessible with no authentication required. The file's own header identified it as containing confidential internal records.

Details have been withheld from this public report due to the sensitivity of the exposed data. Full details available on request to authorized reviewers (e.g. course instructor).

Takeaway: This was the most severe finding in the assessment old/legacy files left in a publicly accessible web directory with no access control represent a critical, independent risk.

Recommendation: Remove all legacy/backup files from publicly accessible web directories immediately, store them outside the web root with access controls, and audit for other forgotten files under deprecated paths.


#Summary of Key Findings

| # | Finding | Severity | Recommendation |
|---|---------|----------|-----------------|
| 1 | SQL injection auth bypass on patient login | Critical | Parameterized queries, input validation, rate limiting |
| 2 | Unauthenticated access to patient lab reports post-bypass | Critical | Server-side authorization on every file request |
| 3 | Directory listing enabled on /patient/ and /staff/ | Medium | Disable directory indexing, move error_log out of web root |
| 4 | Weak/guessable PDF password ("password") | High | Enforce strong, randomized document passwords |
| 5 | robots.txt discloses sensitive paths | Low | Don't rely on robots.txt for access control |
| 6 | Legacy file publicly exposed (details withheld) | Critical | Remove legacy files from public paths, store backups securely |


Burp Suite BruteForce Testing

Overview

After completing the reconnaissance phase, I moved on to testing the web application’s authentication mechanism using Burp Suite.

The purpose of this exercise was to understand how Burp Suite can be used during an authorized penetration test to analyze login requests and perform controlled brute-force testing.

What I Learned

During this phase, I learned how to:

Configure and use Burp Suite Proxy to intercept HTTP/HTTPS requests.
Capture a login request from a web application.
Examine the parameters sent to the server during authentication.
Send captured requests to Intruder for automated testing.
Identify the username and password parameters that could be tested.
Configure payload positions in Burp Suite.
Run a controlled brute-force/password-testing attack within the authorized lab environment.
Compare responses to identify differences between unsuccessful and potentially successful authentication attempts.
Review HTTP status codes, response lengths, and response content when analyzing results.
Understand why weak passwords can create a security risk.

Methodology

The general process I followed was:

Open the authorized target application.
Configure the browser to work through Burp Suite.
Intercept the login request using Proxy.
Examine the request and identify the authentication parameters.
Send the request to Intruder.
Configure the appropriate attack position.
Load the authorized test wordlist/payloads.
Run the controlled attack.
Analyze the responses and identify differences between failed and successful attempts.
Document the findings and evidence.

Key Learning

One of the main things I learned is that Burp Suite is not simply a password cracking tool. It allows a penetration tester to intercept, modify, automate, and analyze web requests.

During brute-force testing, the important part is not only sending many passwords but also understanding how the application’s responses change and what those differences tell the tester.

Security Considerations

Brute-force testing should only be performed against systems where explicit authorization has been provided.

Applications can reduce the risk of brute-force attacks by implementing controls such as:

Strong password policies
Account lockout or temporary delays
Rate limiting
Multi-factor authentication (MFA)
CAPTCHA or bot detection
Monitoring and alerting for repeated failed logins

Tools Used

Burp Suite
Web browser
Authorized test environment
Approved wordlist/payloads

Outcome

This exercise helped me understand the practical workflow of using Burp Suite Intruder to perform controlled authentication testing and analyze web application responses.

It also helped me understand how reconnaissance findings can be followed by a more focused testing phase during a penetration test.




                      
